        <div id="older-models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Older Vision Treadmills: T9200, T9800, TF20, T40 and T80</h2>
<p>
            Most Vision <a href="/treadmills-for-sale/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>treadmills for sale</a> today are older home models from specialty dealers. These are the ones you
            are most likely to meet.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Vision T9200</h3>
          <p>
            The T9200 was an entry home treadmill from the 2000s; parts suppliers list 2002-2003 and 2004 versions, and
            it was sold in Simple, Deluxe and Premier console versions. TreadmillReviews.com's coverage gives a 2.5 HP
            motor, a 325 lb user rating, 12 mph, 12% incline and a non-folding frame for the basic version; a listing for
            the Deluxe gives 3.0 CHP. Because the figures vary by version and year, check the manual for the exact unit.
            Any T9200 is now around two decades old. Parts such as walking belts are still listed by third-party
            suppliers, but it is a machine to buy for very little, not for its name.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">T9800, TF20, T40 and T80</h3>
          <p>
            These are the home models covered by the January 2026 recall, sold from 2012 until May 2025. The TF20 was
            Vision's folding entry model, the T40 a non-folding home treadmill, the T80 the top home model, sold for
            heavy home and light commercial use, and the T9800 an older premium model. The recall notice describes the
            recalled units as having a 60 by 20-inch running platform and a 33-inch-wide console. The 2006 recall of
            Premier consoles, below, covers some T9700-era machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            A late-model T40 or T80 can still be a good used buy, because Johnson Health Tech supports the recall and the
            frames were dealer-grade. Check that the recall power cord has been fitted, and use our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            for the rest. Our <a href="/where-to-buy-a-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to buy a used treadmill</a>
            guide covers dealers' trade-ins and refurbished stock, often the best source of used Vision machines.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recalls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Vision Treadmill Recalls</h2>
<p>
            We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database for Vision Fitness and Johnson Health
            Tech on 5 November 2026. Two recalls cover Vision treadmills.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Recall 26-203, 22 January 2026:</strong> an expansion of an earlier Matrix commercial recall to
            about 47,000 Matrix Retail and Vision home treadmills, including the Vision T9800, TF20, T40 and T80, made
            from June 2007 to January 2024 and sold without a power-cord bracket. The power cord can work loose from the
            treadmill's socket, a fire hazard; Johnson Health Tech had 27 reports of overheating at the connection and no
            injuries. They sold from April 2012 to May 2025 for $2,100 to $8,100. The remedy is a free replacement power
            cord with an automatic locking plug, mailed for installation without tools. Until it is fitted, owners should
            check the cord is fully pushed into the socket before each use.</li>
            <li><strong>Recall 06-556, 12 June 2006:</strong> about 480 Vision Fitness 2006 Premier consoles (models
            TCPP6, TCFP6 and C9700H6, marked Premier or T9700HRT) sold on Vision treadmill frames in September and October
            2005 for $1,700 to $3,000, which could increase speed and incline unexpectedly. One incident, no injuries.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to check a Vision treadmill</h3>
          <p>
            The model number and frame serial number are on the lower front crossbar near the <a href="/how-to-turn-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>power switch</a>. The serial
            begins with TM, then a three-digit model code (TM432 for the TF20, TM433 for the T40, TM445 for the T80,
            TM87 for the T9800), then the build date. Johnson Health Tech's recall page at visionfitness.com lists the
            exact serial ranges, and the recall line is 800-211-4590. If a cord bracket or the new locking cord is
            already fitted, the unit has been dealt with.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What to Buy Instead of a Vision Home Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            If you are shopping new and liked Vision's position — better than big-box quality, dealer support, a long
            frame and motor warranty — the closest current equivalents are:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong><a href="/horizon-7-8-at-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon 7.8 AT</a>,
            $1,999:</strong> the same parent company, a 22 by 60-inch deck, 15% incline and 375 lb, with lifetime frame and
            motor cover. Our <a href="/best-horizon-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best Horizon treadmills</a>
            guide covers the rest of the range.</li>
            <li><strong><a href="/matrix-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Matrix home treadmills</a>:</strong>
            Johnson Health Tech's premium dealer line, closest to what the Vision T80 was. Some older Matrix Retail models
            share the 2026 recall.</li>
            <li><strong><a href="/life-fitness-run-cx-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Life Fitness Run CX</a>,
            $3,919:</strong> a club brand's home treadmill with a 400 lb rating, delivered and installed.</li>
            <li><strong><a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole</a> and
            <a href="/spirit-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Spirit</a>:</strong> both sell dealer-grade
            home treadmills with long warranties in the price band Vision used to occupy.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For buyers set on light-commercial build — a home gym with several users, or a building's fitness room —
            the T600 makes sense, and our guide to <a href="/what-treadmills-do-gyms-use/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what treadmills gyms use</a>
            sets it against Precor, <a href="/life-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Life Fitness</a> and Matrix.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Is Vision on Amazon?</h3>
          <p>
            We found no Vision treadmill sold new by Vision or an authorised dealer on Amazon US, which fits a brand sold
            through local offices and dealers; Amazon searches for Vision mostly return parts. The product cards on this
            page therefore go to the nearest alternatives that are on Amazon. If you want a T600 itself, buy from a dealer
            who will deliver, install and honour the warranty.
          </p>
        </div>

